@@ -18,7 +18,7 @@ Queue records persist in MongoDB with a unique project/finding key, optimistic v
 
 ## Configuration and safeguards
 
-Conductor uses `REMEDY_API_BASE_URL` and `REMEDY_API_KEY` (legacy aliases: `PROJECT_REMEDY_BASE_URL` and `PROJECT_REMEDY_API_KEY`). Deploy the gateway recovery routes and bridge endpoints together with the Conductor changes. Preserve each deployment's environment files, compose overrides, networks, and persistent snapshot volume.
+Conductor requires explicit server opt-in with `REMEDY_ENABLED=true`, `REMEDY_ORG_ID` equal to the deployment's `ORG_ID` and the stored project's `orgID`, plus `REMEDY_API_BASE_URL` and `REMEDY_API_KEY`. Legacy aliases and implicit localhost defaults are no longer accepted. See the tenant configuration policy below. Deploy the gateway recovery routes and bridge endpoints together with the Conductor changes. Preserve each deployment's environment files, compose overrides, networks, and persistent snapshot volume.
 
 Bridge inference settings are documented in its `.env.example`. The shared client supports Ollama primary and OpenAI fallback for text, vision, and advanced agent tool calls. OpenAI tool calls use Responses, with tool-result and reasoning-context replay. Availability failures can trigger fallback; invalid requests and refusals do not.
 
@@ -75,3 +75,56 @@ An 82-page sandbox book was remediated with retained pre-write snapshots: duplic
 A separate controlled alt-text change on an already-remediated page was restored through Conductor. The original long description remained intact; restore rescanned to zero findings and refreshed the matrix. Queue verification remains a reviewer action.
 
 The static scanner has a reproducible jsdom limitation on MathML: even `<math><mi>x</mi></math>` can make axe ARIA checks report a `getComputedStyle` error because the simulated MathML element lacks inline-style support. Related checks remain informational/manual review. Do not suppress these checks or interpret them as a pass; validate mathematical content and rendered-page behavior in a real browser. Keyboard behavior, contrast, and subject-matter adequacy still require reviewer checks.
+
+
+## Tenant configuration and readiness (LT-131)
+
+The server defaults Remedy OFF. For one intended deployment/organization, set
+`REMEDY_ENABLED=true` exactly and set `REMEDY_ORG_ID` to its existing `ORG_ID`.
+Every project is checked against its stored `orgID`; support access to another
+organization's project does not opt that organization in. A process serving more
+than its `ORG_ID` needs a separately approved mapping before enabling more tenants.
+A browser flag never enables these routes.
+
+An enabled deployment must explicitly supply `REMEDY_API_BASE_URL` (an HTTPS
+origin without credentials, path, query or fragment) and `REMEDY_API_KEY`.
+Explicit HTTP loopback origins are accepted for same-host local fixtures;
+there is no localhost fallback in any environment. Redirects are refused.
+Production credential provisioning and deployment remain owner-only steps.
+
+Authenticated `GET /project/accessibility/remedy/health?projectID=...` requires
+current project access. It returns only safe availability/capability fields and
+fixed messages, never the engine address, key or upstream error body. Configured
+preflight checks authenticated `/v1/cxone/health`, requiring `ok` and the scan,
+preview and mutate capabilities; liveness alone cannot enable the UI. Engine
+failure, missing config or optional storage failure leaves ordinary Conductor
+routes usable. Feature requests fail closed with a useful unavailable response.
+The mounted matrix disables scan/fix and does not mount queue/review controls
+until the server reports the project's effective capability. A recheck is
+available to recover from an outage.
+
+Feature models are compiled lazily after a configured, authorized request.
+They explicitly disable automatic collection/index creation. The first enabled
+health preflight or authorized work-queue request creates only `remediationworkitems` and
+`conformancerecords` and builds their declared indexes before exposing readiness:
+unique `{projectID:1,key:1}` for work-item deduplication and
+`{projectID:1,kind:1,createdAt:-1}` for conformance history lookup, plus Mongo `_id`.
+The database principal therefore needs collection/index creation privileges for
+these collections. Duplicates, missing privileges or index failures report
+unavailable; they do not crash unrelated startup or trigger automatic data
+repair. An operator must inspect existing duplicates before enablement; no
+retained rows or indexes are removed. Successful readiness is cached in-process;
+normal database failures continue to fail feature requests, and external index
+changes require an operator preflight/restart before use.
+
+History/evidence arrays and append-only conformance records grow without a new
+retention policy. Back up Projects, work items, conformance records and referenced
+identities consistently; preserve section IDs, fingerprints, evidence versions
+and pending claims. Restore must coordinate with the Bridge operation/snapshot
+ledger and trust epoch. Never clear pending claims or retry a write solely from a
+backup restore. Reconciliation is read-only until a separately authorized,
+audited administrative resolution exists.
+
+This policy is tested with Node22, isolated local Mongo and synthetic external
+providers. Actual Atlas tier, production Mongo/topology, orchestrator, provider
+access, immutable images, rollback and full-stack release gates remain open.
